= Essen, 6 and 7 January

The Allied conference in Paris has broken up. Britain wanted to give Germany time; Poincaré wants the Ruhr, and he is waiting only for the Reparations Commission to declare the next delivery of coal late. In Essen the Comintern brings together the communist parties of Germany, France, Belgium, Britain, Italy, Czechoslovakia and the Netherlands, so that the workers on both sides of the Rhine hear one answer to both governments.

The answer is an appeal: against the French occupation, and against Cuno, who will ask the workers to stand behind their own industrialists. It also calls a second, larger conference of every workers' organisation for the spring. If the SPD and the unions come, the party has a united front where it counts. If they stay away, the workers will see who stayed away.

- @appeal: Issue the joint appeal and invite every workers' organisation to the second conference.
